The user wants to add input handling to the index.html script: "Input handling — create Set keys, add keydown/keyup listeners, tap/click listener for burst, R-key and restart button to call reset()."

Now we need to edit index.html accordingly.

We should add code in the appropriate section ("// === INPUT ==============================================================" maybe). There's no INPUT section yet. We need to create a new placeholder section and add code for handling input. However the example design template includes sections: CONFIG, STATE, RESET, LOOP/UPDATE, RENDER, INPUT, AUDIO, OBSERVABLE CONTRACT, BOOT. In the current code, we have CONFIG and STATE and LOOP sections, but we haven't created RESET etc. Now we need to add input handling, but we might need to also add a reset function placeholder because the input handling references reset(). The task says to add keydown/keyup listeners, tap/click listener for burst, R-key and restart button to call reset(). The presence of reset() is not yet defined; we need to ensure that calling reset() does not cause errors. The page must load without console errors. So we either need to create a minimal reset() function defined somewhere in the script (maybe after input). The task does not ask to create reset function yet (Task 5 later will be "Player movement"). However, we need to call reset() on R or restart click. If reset is not defined, it will cause a ReferenceError. The spec for input handling might implicitly require that reset() exists (since later tasks will define it). However we can defensively define a placeholder reset function that does minimal things (like just stop loop and start again). But the task indicates "Input handling — create Set keys, add keydown/keyup listeners, tap/click listener for burst, R-key and restart button to call reset()." It doesn't say to implement reset. There might be a plan to define reset later (task 5, maybe resetting game). However the page must not have console errors on load. Adding an event listener that calls reset() on button click will trigger only on click, not on load. However there's also an immediate call to reset maybe needed for boot later (task 13 boot). That may be defined later as well. In the current file, there is no boot code, no call to any function. The page currently loads, the script sets up some global variables, defines startLoop, but does not start any loop. That's fine. Input handling will add listeners: keydown/keyup, click/tap.

We need to make sure the code doesn't reference any undefined variables; 'keys' have already been defined as let keys = new Set(); So that's fine.

Now we need to add input handling code: add event listeners for keydown, keyup as shown in reference example:

```
document.addEventListener("keydown", (ev) => {
    if (ev.key === "r" || ev.key === "R") { reset(); return; }
    keys.add(ev.key);
});
document.addEventListener("keyup", (ev) => { keys.delete(ev.key); });
restartBtn.addEventListener("click", reset);
```

Also tap/click listener for burst: Since the player triggers a burst for time dilation. We need to add e.g.:

document.addEventListener("click", (ev) => { /* maybe call triggerBurst() */ });

But from the task: input handling — create Set keys, add keydown/keyup listeners, tap/click listener for burst, R-key and restart button to call reset(). So there must be a click listener that triggers the burst. Probably to something like a function 'activateBurst()', which may not be defined yet. If we call a non-existent function from click, it will cause a reference error on click, but that only triggers when the user clicks. However the page load should not error. However the instruction mentions "tap/click listener for burst". This likely expects us to call a placeholder function like 'burst()' which may be defined later. It might also be okay to set a variable (like canBurst) toggled but not defined yet. However spec says "tap/click listener for burst". Perhaps we add:

document.addEventListener("click", (ev) => {
    // TODO: implement burst activation
    // Placeholder: console.log("burst");
});

Or define a function 'triggerBurst' later and call it if defined? Might just log but not error.

Rather than referencing an undefined function, we could have the click listener do nothing (or set a flag) but still be there. The objective of the task is to set up input handling; later tasks will add the behavior for burst.

Thus we should perhaps add a variable like 'burstRequested = false' and set it to true on click/tap. However the state initialization hasn't defined such variable. Could add it now as part of input handling, as a global variable. Is that acceptable? The tasks haven't defined it but it's a small addition.

But we need to be mindful that future tasks might define burst function expecting to check something else. Simpler approach: just create the click listener that sets a flag, maybe 'burstRequested = true', which we can later handle in input handling or update logic.

Actually the task 6 is "Time‑dilation burst — on tap, apply short forward speed boost to player and temporarily reduce tunnel scroll speed; enforce cooldown timer before next activation." So they will need some mechanism to know when burst is triggered. Could be a variable like burstActive or burstCooldown, etc. But they could also just directly call a function 'activateBurst()' which we will define later. But for now, we can just have a placeholder 'activateBurst' function defined as no-op. That would satisfy later tasks.

Therefore we can add a minimal placeholder function for the burst (maybe in a future section). But we must not add new sections out of order? The example order is "CONFIG → STATE → RESET → LOOP/UPDATE → RENDER → INPUT → AUDIO → OBSERVABLE CONTRACT → BOOT". We yet have sections CONFIG, STATE, LOOP. We should add a RESET section after STATE? However not required for this task. We need an INPUT section; can be after LOOP as per the template order.

Thus let's add an INPUT section after the loop (maybe after the closing brace of startLoop). The earlier script ends with startLoop definition before closing script. Let's modify the script to include an INPUT block after the LOOP block.

